.TH CHMAP_CREATE_FULL 3 "2026-08-25" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_create_full \- create a hash map with full customization
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chashmap.h>

chmap chmap_create_full(size_t initial_bucket_array_size,
                        ccol_data_type key_type,
                        ccol_data_type val_type,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
                        ccol_hashing_proc_t custom_hashing_proc,
                        char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a hash map with full control over initial size, types, memory
management, and hashing function. The implementation strategy is
automatically selected based on key and value types.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
initial_bucket_array_size  Initial number of buckets
key_type                   Type of keys
val_type                   Type of values
mmgmt_procs                Custom memory management, or NULL
custom_hashing_proc        Custom hash function, or NULL
err                        Optional pointer to receive error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to newly created hash map on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Implementation selection:
  - Open-addressing: Both key and value are integral types <=8 bytes
  - Separate chaining: Either key or value is non-integral or >8 bytes
Integral types: char, short, int, long, long long (signed/unsigned),
               float, double
Excludes long double from open-addressing (can be 10-16 bytes)
For a float or double key_type, a -0.0 key is always canonicalized to
0.0 before it is hashed, stored, or compared, matching -0.0 == 0.0 in
C. This applies unconditionally, including when custom_hashing_proc is
non-NULL: the custom function always receives the already-canonicalized
bit pattern, and the canonicalized value is what ends up stored.
For a long double key_type (always separate chaining), key equality
and hashing are based on the key's numeric value rather than its raw
byte representation: -0.0L and 0.0L collide into one key like -0.0/0.0
do for float/double, and two keys holding the identical value remain
the same key even if their platform-defined padding bits differ. Every
NaN long double collapses into a single key, unlike float/double's own
distinct-NaN-payload behavior. This value-based handling is bypassed
when custom_hashing_proc is non-NULL: the custom function receives the
raw bytes (padding included), so a custom hash function for a long
double key type must itself be value-based to avoid spurious lookup
misses.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_create (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
